  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 253 PM EDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nA ridge axis remains centered well to our west while bouts of energy \nwill rotate through mean flow aloft this afternoon and tonight. This \nwill amplify troughing over the Mid-Atlantic into the southern \nAppalachians. H85 and higher altitude flow will see minor \nenhancements in response to the increasing vorticity advection \ntonight. Because of this, the HREF suite depicts developing \nscattered showers and storms across the region. \n\nThere will be a low risk of isolated flooding through the night. \nAspects of the atmosphere in favor of isolated flooding will be high \nfreezing-levels, anomalous PWATs, skinny CAPE profiles, and a few \nlocations that remain with poor antecedent soil conditions. Working \nagainst the threat of isolated flooding is storm motion and lack of \na surface boundary to train along. Additionally, latest RAP and \nNAMBufr soundings both depict a rather dry atmosphere at ~550mb and \nhigher, suggesting limited depth of saturation.\n\nSunday afternoon looks to be mostly dry, though an isolated shower \nor storm cannot be totally ruled out. By Sunday night into early \nMonday morning CAMs are suggest a MCS progressing through northern \nKY and southward through portions of central and East Tennessee. \nThis activity moves quite quick so flooding should not be of \nconcern. The main focus will be keeping an eye on the potential for \nsome strong to damaging wind gusts. NAM soundings depict a fairly \nstout low-level inversion so confidence is low that damaging gusts \ncould make it to the surface. Will have to keep an eye on how the \npotential CAPE gradient plays out alongside timing trends. \n\nA more potent shortwave is expected to promote development of an \nupper low over the eastern Great Lakes and Mid-Atlantic Tuesday into \nWednesday. A surface low develops in response, and an associated \ncold front will bring increasing chances for showers and storms. \nNAMBufr soundings have quite an impressive thermodynamic profile at \nleast for Tuesday afternoon, but timing of the storms may not \nnecessarily work out to tap into peak heating and the latest hi-\nresolution guidance does not quite make it into those overnight \nhours. LREF joint probabilities of MUCAPE 1500+ J/kg and shear \n30+kts are generally around 40 to 60% in our northeastern locations \nwhere a day 4 Slight risk is currently in place. Primary hazard \nwould be damaging winds. \n\nRegarding temperatures, Sunday through Tuesday will lean hot & humid.\nHeat indices will be greatest Monday and Tuesday, with near to low \n100 degree values expected to creep into the central valley. Post \nFROPA, mostly dry and slightly cooler/less humid conditions are \nexpected mid to late week.",
